HOW TO USE THIS FILE
--------------------
Work the problems in order. A problem is done when every box under
"Turn in" is checked. Do not start the next problem early.

The order book library is already built. These problems put a network
and a page in front of it. The reference is TradingEngine:

  frontend/                         Next.js page, port 3000
  backend/                          Go gateway, port 8000
  backend/engine/Server.cpp         C++ HTTP engine, ports 6060+

This plan keeps that shape. FastAPI replaces the Go gateway. The C++
engine is a new program that links the existing orderbook library.
The page talks only to the gateway.


WHAT THIS SYSTEM DOES
---------------------
A person opens the website and runs a simulation: one or more symbols,
how many bids and asks, and a price and quantity range.

  browser  --JSON-->  FastAPI :8000  --HTTP-->  C++ engine per symbol
                                              each engine owns one Orderbook

The gateway assigns order ids, generates the random orders, starts an
engine process if that symbol does not have one, resets the book, and
sends that symbol's orders to its engine. The engine matches them.
The gateway returns, per symbol, the trade count, volume, orders left
on each side, best prices, and level counts, plus how long the parallel
send took. The page shows that.

Single trade, cancel, status, reset, health, and the engine list are
gateway endpoints too. The reference page does not have forms for them.
This plan matches that: the page runs simulations; the other actions
are API calls.


DECISIONS
---------
- New code lives in this repo: orderbook/server, gateway/, frontend/.
- One C++ process per symbol, one Orderbook inside that process.
  The gateway owns the symbol-to-port map. The engine does not keep
  a map of books.
- The order book library still does no I/O. HTTP and JSON stay in the
  server program.
- JSON is parsed with nlohmann/json. Do not copy Server.cpp's
  hand-written parser.
- A missing best price is JSON null. The reference engine sends -1
  and the Go gateway converts it. Null matches the page's types.
- Do not copy the Go fallback that sends an unknown symbol's trade to
  port 6060. /order/trade and /order/cancel start that symbol's engine,
  or return an error if it cannot start.
- Spawn engines for different symbols in parallel. The reference Go
  manager holds a lock across the whole spawn and accidentally runs
  them one at a time.
- Time only the parallel batch send, as Simulation.go does. Do not
  include process startup in executionTimeMs.


